You can use `GET DIAGNOSTICS` within a PL/pgSQL block to retrieve information about the last executed query.

Here is how you can check the row count and the routine OID:

```sql
DO $$
DECLARE
    v_call_stack TEXT;
    v_row_count INTEGER;
    v_OID OID;
BEGIN
    PERFORM * FROM product;
    GET DIAGNOSTICS v_row_count = ROW_COUNT;
    GET DIAGNOSTICS v_OID = PG_ROUTINE_OID;
    GET DIAGNOSTICS v_call_stack = PG_CONTEXT;

    RAISE NOTICE 'Routine OID %', v_OID;
    RAISE NOTICE 'Call stack: %', v_call_stack;

    IF FOUND THEN
        RAISE NOTICE 'Query successful. Found % rows', v_row_count;
    ELSE
        RAISE NOTICE 'Nothing found';
    END IF;
END;
$$;
```

`ROW_COUNT` will hold the number of rows affected by the query, and `PG_ROUTINE_OID` will provide the OID of the current function.

